A CPU and 8 GB GPU can run a 16 GB quantized model by partitioning weights. The CPU’s main memory stores the full 16 GB, while the GPU keeps only a working set: the layers needed for the current forward pass, plus activations and KV cache. Before a layer executes, its weights are copied over PCIe from CPU to GPU; after use, they may be evicted or kept if hot. To hide latency, the system prefetches the next layer while the current one computes, using double buffering.

A practical scheduling trade-off is how much weight to pin in GPU memory. Pinning more weights reduces PCIe transfers and improves throughput, but consumes VRAM that could hold larger batches, longer sequences, or KV cache. Pinning less preserves memory headroom but increases transfer stalls. The scheduler chooses a resident set that balances transfer cost against compute and memory pressure.
